A||N||7||Formula88||Different Fuel Gauge Problem||||5||
Z||000000||Formula88||06-27-2001||03:07 PM||||Ok, ever since I got my Fiero, empty is about 1/8th tank on the gauge.  But today, for some reason, it started sputtering like it was about to run out of gas at 1/4 tank.  So, I pull in, get some gas, and voila, no more sputter.  I know the gauge isn't correct, but what could make the deviation change all of a sudden?  The changes to the car during this last tank of gas are:<P>Rodney's 180* thermostat and fan switch<BR>Oil Change (switched from 5W30 to 10W30)<BR>fixed exhaust leak.<BR>repaired headlight motor.<P>Nothing here should effect the fuel gauge (fan switch?)  I don't see how the fan switch could affect it, but it's the only electrical change to the car.<P>Ideas?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||ray b||06-27-2001||04:05 PM||||the pointer is just stuck on the pivot shaft<BR>at the factory it can and does slip and move to a new position <BR>take off the cover and pull the pointer off the shaft and reposition to read right amt in tank glue it back on one drop only' your chose the glue. super or other type<BR>full tank is best befor you replace it but will work empty to but then your out of gas<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||06-27-2001||04:30 PM||||That sounds like too easy a fix.  One thing I'm concerned about is that the needles are very flimsy and can easily be broken.  I'm hesitant to just yank on it to get it off.  Is there anything holding it on other than friction?  Any special technique other than "be careful"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000003||StuGood||06-27-2001||04:44 PM||||Not sure, but my guess is it's just a coincidence.  Apparently the sending units go bad, when coils on the fuel gage rheostat (in the tank) come unglued and start moving around...  which sounds as if it could happen anytime.<P>I don't have experience with the tank units, but the rheostats in the oil pressure senders   can go whacky that way.  Since the fuel gage circuit is balanced, it shouldn't be affected by variations in battery voltage much... and how the fan switch could have anything to do with the fuel gage is beyond me.   <P>If you have an extra fuel gage, could swap it out to eliminate that possibility (less trouble than dropping fuel tank), or test the dash gage using resistances described in Oliver's (I think) Fiero page.  <P>...the voice of inexperience  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> says it's probably the tank unit.  Fortunately, I've been lucky enough to avoid fuel gage problems so far.  <P>But since the gage was innaccurate already, I tend to wonder if tank unit is getting worse.     ||||reg||5
Z||000004||Formula88||06-27-2001||04:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by StuGood:<BR><B>But since the gage was innaccurate already, I tend to wonder if tank unit is getting worse.     </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, it was as accurate as ALL Fiero fuel gauges.  It just changed today.  Before it was always empty right at 1/8th tank.  I'll keep an eye on it and see if the empty point keeps changing.  I really don't want to drop the tank, if I can avoid it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||06-27-2001||10:37 PM||||all the needles are just press on. just make sure you pull it straight out rite at pivot point. some are really tough, I almost had to brace my foot against dash to get speedo needle off ( <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">...)I would suggest you dont glue it, if you do, it never will come back off, and if a drop gets in the works the gauge will be worthless.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ray b||06-27-2001||10:37 PM||||pointer will come off with fingers pressure<BR>only.grab the hub only its loose or you don't have a problem.<BR>if it reads over full when toped off and runs out at 1/8 1/4 or 1/3 or more mark it is a wandering pointer, not in tank problem<BR>you can just move it by hand, by the hub not the needle and or push it on the shaft harder but i like to glue it so i don't have to do it again.i have done it to 3 cars now. very cheap fix<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd<p>[This message has been edited by ray b (edited 06-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||06-28-2001||09:32 AM||||Cool.  That just got added to my Fiero Weekend list this week.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
